# Suite observability: Windows Event Log + syslog (hard requirement)

Operator directive (2026-08): **tracking of all applications in this suite is a
hard requirement.** Every shipping agent and component MUST generate events to
native Windows Event Log **and** MUST be able to generate syslog events.
Tracing alone is not enough.

## The rule

> **Every shipping Windows binary in the AIC PIM suite emits operational and
> security-relevant events through `pim-windows-oplog` (registered Event Source
> + shared or product message DLL). Every shipping binary — Windows, Linux, and
> macOS — implements an RFC 5424 syslog sink wired to `app-config` keys so
> operators can enable remote forwarding. Default may be off; the implementation
> is required. Manifest keys without a real sink are forbidden.**

1. **Windows Event Log (WEL)** — process lifecycle, startup failure, and
   security-relevant operator / audit actions go through
   `pim_windows_oplog::emit` / `report_*` / `AuditEvent` (or the
   `pim_app_config::windows_oplog` re-export). The Event Source is registered
   (installer and/or self-heal at process start). Message text resolves via the
   shared `pim_windows_oplog_messages.dll` and/or a product message catalog.
2. **Syslog (RFC 5424)** — each shipping binary can forward the same class of
   events to a remote collector when configured. Keys live in that product's
   `app-config.toml` (for example `SYSLOG_ENABLED`, `SYSLOG_HOST`,
   `SYSLOG_PORT`, `SYSLOG_PROTOCOL`, facility, ident, TLS CA / SNI). Default may
   be `false` / empty host; the code path, configurator fields, and diagnostics
   probe must exist.
3. **Cross-platform** — on Linux/macOS, WEL is N/A; syslog (and/or journald /
   unified-log mirrors documented per product) still applies. "Windows-only
   suite" is not an excuse to skip syslog on Unix daemons.
4. **AU-\* alignment** — this operationalizes
   [`enterprise-compliance.mdc`](enterprise-compliance.mdc) §5 (NIST SP 800-53
   AU-2, AU-3, AU-9, AU-12): auditable events reach native OS and SIEM-friendly
   channels, not only rolling `tracing` files.

## Applies to (shipping products)

WEL required on Windows **and** RFC5424 syslog required for: AIC Server
(`pim-offline-server` and service hosts); endpoint agent
(`pim-offline-agent` / client); recording agent and evidence writers; Workspace
Agent (WA) and related shipping agents; database management (`db-mgmt` and
shipping GUIs/CLIs); jump / session / recording proxy capacity binaries that
ship; all shipping configurators (Win32 + Tauri) and elevate helpers (probe +
config keys; forward security-relevant actions); and every future shipping suite
binary.

Internal libraries, one-shot build tools, and non-shipping examples are out of
scope unless packaged into a customer-facing EXE.

## Required patterns

```rust
// At process start (after fatal config checks where appropriate):
pim_windows_oplog::report_process_start_auto();
let _stop = pim_windows_oplog::ProcessStopGuard::new();

// Security-relevant action:
pim_windows_oplog::emit(ids::SOME_AUDIT_ID, &payload)?;
// or AuditEvent builder — never invent ad-hoc ReportEventW bypasses.
```

Each **binary-producing** crate links the message resource from its own
`build.rs` (see `pim-windows-oplog/docs/WINDOWS_OPERATIONAL_EVENT_LOG.md`).
Installers register the Event Source; runtime may self-heal `EventMessageFile`
drift.

For syslog: declare `SYSLOG_*` (or product-prefixed `syslog_enabled` /
`syslog_host`) in `app-config.toml`; construct a real sink at startup / settings
refresh that sends RFC 5424 when enabled; provide an operator diagnostics probe
(configurator and/or admin API) proving reachability without a production SIEM;
honor [`no-environment-variables.mdc`](no-environment-variables.mdc) —
configuration comes from `pim-app-config`, never `$env:SYSLOG_*`.

## Forbidden constructs

- **Tracing-only stubs for "event ids"** — constants, enums, or log lines naming
  WEL / syslog event IDs without calling `pim-windows-oplog` (Windows) or the
  syslog sink (when enabled).
- **Unused `EVENTS_*` / `SYSLOG_*` / `event_log_*` / `syslog_*` manifest keys**
  with no fan-out sink constructed at runtime.
- **Bypassing `pim-windows-oplog`** with raw `ReportEventW` / `EventLog::write`
  that skips the shared catalog, redaction helpers, and `declared_event_ids()`
  self-test.
- **"We'll add syslog later"** for a new shipping product — the sink (even
  default-off) lands in the same change set as the binary's first
  customer-facing release path.
- Logging secret plaintext into WEL or syslog — mask per
  [`enterprise-compliance.mdc`](enterprise-compliance.mdc) §5
  (`pim_app_config::windows_oplog::redact::*`).

WEL writes remain best-effort relative to the operator-visible action (AU-9:
record the audit-write failure to the diagnostic log and proceed). That does
**not** excuse missing the sink implementation.

